<!-- Section 6: Data Limitations & Recommendations -->
<div class="section" id="data-limitations">
  <h2>6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>

  <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> This dataset is an unbalanced panel &mdash; not all 201 countries report data in every year. The composition of reporting countries changes annually, making year-over-year comparisons of aggregate statistics potentially misleading. A declining median could partly reflect the addition of low-rate countries into the dataset rather than genuine improvement.</li>
    <li><strong>Coverage decline in 2020s:</strong> Reporting coverage fell from 151 countries (2007) to just 84 (2023). This likely reflects COVID-era disruptions to statistical systems and reporting delays, but it introduces selection bias into recent trend estimates.</li>
    <li><strong>United Kingdom caveat:</strong> UK data refer to England and Wales only. Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported separately and should not be aggregated with the UK figure.</li>
    <li><strong>Territories and small states:</strong> Territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) are reported separately from their parent countries. Small island states can exhibit extreme rate volatility due to small denominators &mdash; a single incident can dramatically shift the rate.</li>
    <li><strong>Age-disaggregated data gaps:</strong> Age-specific data is only available for male and female categories (not "both"), and coverage is more limited than overall data. Some age groups lack sufficient data for cross-decade comparison.</li>
    <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing data was not interpolated. All statistics presented reflect only the available observations, not estimated values for missing country-years.</li>
    <li><strong>Underreporting:</strong> Homicide data depends on national criminal justice systems. Countries with weak institutions may undercount homicides, creating a systematic bias that conflates institutional capacity with actual violence levels.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Target the Caribbean and Central America:</strong> The data overwhelmingly identifies the Caribbean and Central America as the subregions most in need of targeted intervention. International cooperation on arms trafficking, drug trade disruption, and criminal justice capacity-building should be prioritized for these areas.</li>
    <li><strong>Address male victimization among working-age adults:</strong> The persistent 3:1 male-to-female homicide ratio, concentrated in the 15&ndash;44 age range, calls for targeted violence prevention programs for young men &mdash; including gang exit programs, economic opportunity initiatives, and community-based conflict resolution.</li>
    <li><strong>Learn from successful decliners:</strong> Countries like El Salvador, Estonia, Georgia, and Singapore achieved dramatic reductions. While context-specific, systematic study of their policy approaches &mdash; institutional reform, community policing, socioeconomic development &mdash; could inform strategies elsewhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Monitor Ecuador and Costa Rica:</strong> These two countries stand out as large nations where homicide rates have increased significantly after historically lower levels. Early-warning systems and rapid-response interventions may help reverse these trends before they become entrenched.</li>
    <li><strong>Invest in data infrastructure:</strong> The declining reporting coverage &mdash; particularly in the 2020s &mdash; undermines evidence-based policymaking. International organizations should prioritize supporting national statistical systems, especially in under-reporting regions, to ensure the global community can track progress reliably.</li>
    <li><strong>Adopt a balanced-panel approach for monitoring:</strong> To control for composition effects, policymakers should track a consistent set of countries over time (a balanced panel) alongside the full-sample aggregates. This would distinguish genuine rate changes from artifacts of changing reporting coverage.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

